<!--Post: 12142--><!--Time: 1111644216-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12142"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 23, 2005 - 10:03 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->&#34;Itty&#34;: I don&#39;t know if the animal that was with one of those &#34;dudes&#34; was a duck. But! If it looked like a duck and walked like a duck, and then even &#39;quacked&#39; like a duck, you can be pretty sure it WAS in fact, a duck. <BR> <BR>ALSO: Hint for anyone getting their &#34;BTK&#34; book autographed by Robert &#40;&#34;Notorious B.I.G.&#34;&#41; Beattie in person: <BR> <BR>Do yourself a favor &amp; have him autograph the FRONT COVER! I know from lots of experience that having the cover itself signed by the author is a much better collectors&#39; item. It definitely makes for an infinitely better keepsake to show off to others. Having an autograph on one of the inside pages is &#34;invisible&#34; and relegates the autograph to a kind of &#34;footnote&#34; status. It has to be carefully searched for in order to even know it exists. Whereas the signature put proudly on the cover is visible to all &amp; makes for a great showpiece on your mantle or wall, etc. <BR> <BR>Victor<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12142-->
<!--Post: 12150--><!--Time: 1111671632-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12150"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Juno<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Juno<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->5-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, March 24, 2005 - 5:40 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Actually, it&#39;s better if the author inscribes the title page, leaving the dust jacket pristine. Another advantage is that if your dust jacket gets damaged you may be able to replace it, thus still having a &#34;mint&#34; signed book.  In addition, the signature becomes part of the book, and, in the case of a rare collection, will be included in the catalog entry. <BR> <BR>When signed editions are released the signature is NEVER on the dust jacket.  It is either on the title page ot a seperate page for the number and signature only.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12150-->
<!--Post: 12169--><!--Time: 1111695016-->
