<!--Post: 12448--><!--Time: 1112236362-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12448"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Megabyte<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Megabyte<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, March 30, 2005 - 6:32 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->here is a link to my own discussion board that I have set up: <a href="http://b3.boards2go.com/boards/board.cgi?user=newswebmaster" target=_top>http://b3.boards2go.com/boards/board.cgi?user=news webmaster</a> <BR> <BR>and by the looks of it, it looks like somebody from the net has been posting alot<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12448-->
<!--Post: 12588--><!--Time: 1112504321-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12588"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 02, 2005 - 8:58 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Man...I&#39;m mid-way through <i>Nightmare in Wichita</i>, y&#39;all, and I&#39;ve been making notes - I do this when I am planning to review a book at <a href="http://blogcritics.org">blogcritics.</a> <BR> <BR>I appreciate that Mr. Beattie has worked so hard to write this book. There are revelations, yes. I&#39;ve gained insight I didn&#39;t have before already.  <BR> <BR>But without giving you all an preview of the review, let me just say, lawyer Beattie was not done justice by his proofreaders or editors. I&#39;d welcome any further comments on this.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12588-->
<!--Post: 12603--><!--Time: 1112544701-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12603"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 9:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Is there a way to contact Mr Beattie? <BR>I want to ask him is he saw &#34;the 3 dudes&#34; names when he was doing his research. <BR> <BR>If someone in the know can tell me that they didnt <BR>have any raders in custody in oct 74, I will drop the whole subject.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12603-->
<!--Post: 12605--><!--Time: 1112547234-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12605"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 9:53 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I find it interesting that the &#39;authorities&#39; put the Oteros dog down. He was the only living witness. We have lost too many rights as citizens. Sorry, but that makes me angry. Had not the children already suffered enough loss? The dog may have bitten the killer or torn a piece of clothing from him. Of course, hind sight is always better.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12605-->
<!--Post: 12607--><!--Time: 1112552179-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12607"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 11:16 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><i>&#34;...We have lost too many rights as citizens...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>I&#39;m not disagreeing there, but...the dog got put down back in 1974, Teardrop. Do you mean you think we were losing those rights even then? Just a little confused.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12607-->
<!--Post: 12609--><!--Time: 1112553164-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12609"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Chiquitah<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Chiquitah<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 11:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Sorry to be late on this....but a DUCK? The &#34;DUDE&#34; was trying to play duck-duck-goose?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12609-->
<!--Post: 12612--><!--Time: 1112561704-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12612"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 1:55 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Les Anderson, WSU journalism professor, wrote a review of Robert Beattie&#39;s book. <BR>-Deb <BR> <BR><a href="http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/entertainment/books/11296759.htm" target=_top>http://www.kansas.com/mld/kansas/entertainment/boo ks/11296759.htm</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12612-->
<!--Post: 12619--><!--Time: 1112589050-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12619"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 03, 2005 - 9:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Thanks for posting that link Deb -  <BR> <BR>I am glad to see that Anderson says there will be a corrected book issued later. The errors, he says, are minor...personally I find some of them jarring, actually, but that could just be me. It is, I think, pretty exhaustively researched, and I&#39;m gaining some new insight. <BR> <BR>Especially the following; From Chapter 13, &#34;Burglary on Pinecrest&#34;, about the break in at Anna Williams&#39;s house.  <BR> <BR>Pg. 118: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...In the drawing&#40;...&#41;She is </i>&#40;depicted as&#41;<i> gagged. Most disturbingly, the victim rests with her face toward us, so we can see that her eyes apparently have mirrors over them...&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Now we may know where Thomas Harris inserted real facts learned when researching serial murder for the writing of his novel, <i>Red Dragon.</i> It is known that Harris did research by interviewing actual profilers, and I&#39;ve always thought the &#34;Tooth Fairy&#34; murders in that novel had a BTK-ish element. I am fairly sure that I&#39;ve read one of them was John Douglas, who had access to the BTK files - Douglas, by the way, in retrospect, seems to have been one of the closer-to-the-mark profilers where BTK was concerned. <BR> <BR>Remember, it&#39;s serial killer Frances Dolarhyde who in Harris&#39;s &#39;Dragon&#39; <i>&#34;...enters the houses of sleeping suburban families and butchers them, then puts <b>shards of mirrors in their eyes</b> so he can see his &#39;reflected glory&#39;...&#34;</i> - this tidy summation found in a review of the movie version of that novel made in 2002. The review can be found <a href="http://slate.msn.com/id/2071909/">here, in <i>Slate</i></a>. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m sorry, that detail is just too particular for me to think it came out of Harris&#39;s head. He saw files and did exactly what I think I&#39;d probably do in his situation, inserted some of the most strange and telling details into his fiction.  <BR> <BR>It appears that Rader, like many serials, is a big consumer of suspense, mystery, detective fiction - I have little doubt he&#39;s read Thomas Harris&#39;s well-written and painstakingly constructed novels. <i>&#40;Actually, I don&#39;t think &#34;Hannibal&#34; was all that good, but &#39;Dragon&#39; and &#39;Silence&#39; are classics.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>If he did, and he intended to show Anna Williams with mirrors on her eyes in that drawing, don&#39;t you think he just about peed his pants to see that detail used in fiction; in a best-selling novel and <b>two</b> major movies? <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12619-->
<!--Post: 12625--><!--Time: 1112620106-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12625"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sidewalker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sidewalker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 6:08 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I am reading it also. I was surprised to find it in Walmart for ten and some change. <BR>I can well imagine the terror of the public, after the news came out with the fact that there was a serial killer on the loose. <BR> <BR>odd to me also, that the public went nuts with buying alarm systems. Little did they know, the dude worked for an alarm company. Ironic.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12625-->
<!--Post: 12643--><!--Time: 1112663279-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12643"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 6:07 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I decided to purchase Beattie&#39;s literary masterpiece this morning through the internet. I should receive it in a few days and intend to enjoy it, despite some errors made by the author or the publisher noted by members of this message board.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12643-->
<!--Post: 12645--><!--Time: 1112664969-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12645"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, April 04, 2005 - 6:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Ivy, I&#39;m going to make a guess that you will enjoy it for the same reason I am - jarring errors notwithstanding, there <i>is</i> new information in it, and there are conclusions one can draw that I never would have made otherwise. Some of them eye-opening. I don&#39;t want to be cryptic, but it will definitely open your eyes if you keep questions you had throughout the investigation in mind.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12645-->
<!--Post: 12686--><!--Time: 1112791849-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12686"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 5:50 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Just finished the book - a few preliminary thoughts before I write a review later today - Beattie went on to acknowledge Thomas Harris&#39;s use of the &#34;mirrors on eyes&#34; detail later in the book. Whatcha wanna guess was among Denny&#39;s favorite DVDs? <i>&#40;Book doesn&#39;t say that, I&#39;m just wondering aloud.&#41;</i> <BR> <BR>I like Bob Beattie much better than I thought I would upon finishing the book, but I am not fond of his handlers as it relates to rushing this thing to print. It&#39;s kind of a catch-22; I&#39;m glad the book is available, it will always be the volume to beat as to comprehensive coverage of the investigation, but man, is it uneven. Before you say, <i>&#34;dude, that&#39;s true crime for ya, don&#39;t go looking for Pulitzer material,&#34;</i> I have two titles for you; <i>In Cold Blood</i> and <i>The Executioner&#39;s Song</i>. Both true crime tomes, both masterpieces. James Ellroy&#39;s <i>My Dark Places</i> applies, as well.  <BR> <BR>I&#39;m saying I don&#39;t blame most of the book&#39;s faults on Beattie, at the end - the book as a whole suffers from lack of comprehensive editorial oversight and proofing. The narrative tone is very uneven. It&#39;s obvious they did what Beattie told Les Anderson they did - opt for the big sales and plan on a more polished 2nd edition.  <BR> <BR>Beattie&#39;s at his strongest when he goes ahead and speaks directly to the reader. When he opts for any kind of &#34;reportorial&#34; tone, the narrative loses it&#39;s appeal and rhythm. Another book I read last year, <i>Invisible Eden</i> by Maria Flook, about the unsolved murder of fashion writer Christa Worthington, had the opposite problem, I recall - I didn&#39;t like it when Flook spoke directly to the reader, she was better when she was in a more detached mode. But with Beattie, you get a better feel for the impact BTK has had on Wichitans and the victims when he tells directly of stories he heard firsthand. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m giving away the farm now, as to my review - yeah, you&#39;ll probably see part of this post in there.  <BR> <BR>Peculiar to this site and board though, I must note the <b>utter lack</b> of this site&#39;s presence in that book. It&#39;s really pointed, the way the writer ignored catchbtk.com. Due to the considerable amount of lip service given to the C &amp; J board the reason becomes obvious, given the enmity between Runes and Sisu and Tom, and Beattie&#39;s obvious friendship with Bill Peterson - Runes1.  <BR> <BR>Objectively I do find Crime &amp; Justice to be <i>one</i> of the best repositories of BTK info on the web, but I&#39;ve always disliked the layout of the site, how difficult I find it to go back and find old stuff. I think, too, that occasionally folks are almost too nice to certain problem posters, and it bites them on the butt. Or it has, in the past. <BR> <BR>I&#39;m just pretty sure that catchbtk has the top google ranking and I do, from a webmaster&#39;s point of view, like the way Tom designed the site. Seems like a bit much, acting as if this site doesn&#39;t even exist. <i>&#40;For the record, the thing about how you can&#39;t type Tom&#39;s name or link this site from C &amp; J has also always struck me as just a bit too much. Far as I know, Tom could care less who we link from here.&#41;</i> <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12686-->
<!--Post: 12687--><!--Time: 1112793063-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12687"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Sidewalker<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Sidewalker<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->1-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 6:11 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, I totally agree about this site not even being mentioned. Im glad I wasnt the only one, who noticed that. <BR>That other board is an ezboard, which I am used to posting at. &#40;not THAT particular board, but others that are just goof off boards&#41;  <BR>I also agree with your comments re: the book. I read it fast enough, but it was hard to read parts, as it just didnt seem to fit together well.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12687-->
<!--Post: 12688--><!--Time: 1112800179-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12688"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 8:09 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text--><a href="http://blogcritics.org/archives/2005/04/06/110126.php">Book Review at blogcritics.org by me - <i>Nightmare in Wichita: The Hunt For The BTK Strangler, by Robert Beattie</i>.</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12688-->
<!--Post: 12689--><!--Time: 1112804942-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12689"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Itty<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Itty<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 9:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very nice Steve, now spill the &#34;new info&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12689-->
<!--Post: 12690--><!--Time: 1112807033-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12690"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 10:03 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Here&#39;s one thing that comes clear; listening to the 911 call about Nancy Fox, I was always struck by the sound of two operators on the line. I remember thinking it odd that there were two <i>dispatchers.</i> <BR> <BR>There weren&#39;t. BTK/Rader says all that &#34;You will find a home-icide&#34; stuff and you hear the 911 operator say, &#34;I&#39;m sorry sir I didn&#39;t hear you&#34; or something to that effect, then the other operator says &#34;843 South Pershing,&#34; very clearly. <BR> <BR>Beattie reveals that the regular SW Bell operator may have screwed things up, unwittingly - pg 75: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;The </i>&#40;emergency&#41;<i> dispatcher was <b>trained to keep the caller on the line. Because the number and location appeared on her screen, she was simultaneously trying to dispatch a patrol car to the payphone</b>&#40;...&#41; even as she said,</i> &#34;I&#39;m sorry sir. I can&#39;t understand you. What is the address?&#34;&#40;...&#41;<i>the killer had called the operator and asked to be connected to the police. The operator was still on the line. The telephone operator was trained to be helpful and include information and get callers off the phone as soon as possible. She injected herself into the conversation&#40;...&#41;&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>Other new stuff - well, you read about sicko Heidi in my review, didn&#39;t you? She wanted Beattie - this was last year after BTK re-emerged - to arrange an <i>assignation</i> between herself and BTK. I&#39;ll post more when I get more time.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12690-->
<!--Post: 12691--><!--Time: 1112814409-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12691"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 12:06 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The paragraph that really took me back - was where the LE investigated anyone within a square mile of each victims home. If they would have considered Marine Hedge&#39;s murder connected to BTK &#40;like we always knew they should of&#41; he would have been investigated in 1985 and possibly apprehended. This just makes me sick all over again!<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12691-->
<!--Post: 12693--><!--Time: 1112829962-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12693"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lostdog<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lostdog<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 4:26 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->“And if I&#39;m ever in Wichita, I&#39;d like to treat Robert Beattie to coffee and a hot fudge cake at Jerry&#39;s. I&#39;m sure he has more stories to tell.” <BR> <BR>Steve, <BR> <BR>I’m stuck on page one hundred – busy, busy dog! <BR> <BR>I made time to read your review. While I haven’t gotten to Beattie’s mention of various websites, I did scan the last chapter, “Status as of March 2005” &#40;bad habit of mine&#41;.  <BR>I noticed the absence of Tom Voigt’s name. <BR> <BR>……… <BR> <BR>I’m sure Beattie has more stories too. <BR> <BR>Here’s why.  <BR> <BR>I read somewhere that Beattie’s editor trimmed the text by a couple hundred pages. <BR>Still, I doubt that a mention of CatchBTK ended up on the cutting room floor. <BR> <BR>Did petty politics and juvenile internet feuds lead Beattie to omit a reference to the BTK site with the highest number of hits, the greatest level of civility and a sinister poster? <BR> <BR>If so, what other “stories” did the author choose to ignore? <BR> <BR>Perhaps Beattie simply believed that “Catch” was a bag of gas… a folly unworthy of his scrutiny. <BR> <BR>When the fact that Dennis Rader posted on CBTK is revealed and the interplay between TV and LE is disclosed – will Beattie’s coy oversight weaken his work’s stature as BTK canon? <BR> <BR>Tom…er… I mean TIME will tell. <BR> <BR>Oh, one more thing… <BR> <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley Relford <BR>Shirley RELFORD! <BR> <BR>There… I feel a little bit better. <BR> <BR>“I’m sorry that I wasn’t more sensitive” <BR> <BR>LostBob <BR> <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12693-->
<!--Post: 12698--><!--Time: 1112841880-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12698"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, April 06, 2005 - 7:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Doggy, I think you need a little scratchin&#39; behind the ears, bruh...but you know, that &#34;Shirley Relford&#34; was needed - it mirrored my feeling about that. As I said in my review; if you aren&#39;t going to honor simple, touching requests <i>&#40;I thought they were touching&#41;</i> by family members of the victims, don&#39;t tell us they asked. It&#39;s a small quibble about an otherwise decent and important book. <BR> <BR>I agree with you here, too: <BR> <BR><i>&#34;...Did petty politics and juvenile internet feuds lead Beattie to omit a reference to the BTK site with the highest number of hits, the greatest level of civility and a sinister poster?&#34;</i> <BR> <BR>But truthfully, that part about &#34;juvenile internet feuds&#34; is the only part I find myself annoyed with where Beattie is concerned. I&#39;m in an odd position, sorta, because I&#39;ve posted plenty at C &amp; J too, and there are things I really like about that forum, and I think highly of Bill Peterson - but I think if they had such a problem with Tom, all they needed to do was ban him personally and be done with it. The fact that all posters are enforced to not link or even mention Tom is just odd, to me.  <BR> <BR>I do think for the reasons you listed this site merited a mention in the book, and it would have widened the scope, but didn&#39;t feel including this fell within the parameters of my review. <BR> <BR>Now, you need a scooby snack, m&#39; man.  <BR> <BR>What this board needs is a virtual mystery machine with Tom Voigt up front wearing a white shirt with a blue collar and a snappy orange ascot. I volunteer to be Shaggy - he was the best gymnast at their high school, and I was a decent acrobat and dancer back then myself. Way, way back then. <IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12698-->
<!--Post: 12922--><!--Time: 1113764302-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12922"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Flytodeb<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Flytodeb<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->6-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, April 17, 2005 - 11:58 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Wichita&#39;s hottest ticket <BR> <BR>Space is tight for hearing in alleged killer Dennis Rader&#39;s case. <BR> <BR>By Laura Bauer  <BR>Knight Ridder Newspapers <BR> <BR>KANSAS CITY, Mo. When Dennis Rader appears Tuesday in a Sedgwick County courtroom, it could be the beginning of a preliminary hearing like no other in Kansas history.  <BR> <BR>The defendant, who was arrested Feb. 25 and labeled Wichita&#39;s BTK killer by police, has become a household name. The crimes Rader is accused of already are detailed in a book on, The New York Times best-seller list.  <BR> <BR>rest of article called &#34;Wichita&#39;s hottest ticket&#34; here: <BR><a href="http://www.presstelegram.com/Stories/0%2C1413%2C204~21474~2820584%2C00.html" target=_top>http://www.presstelegram.com/Stories/0,1413,204~21 474~2820584,00.html</a><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12922-->
<!--Post: 12991--><!--Time: 1113923873-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12991"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 8:17 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve - Rocky and ALL on this site.  I don&#39;t understand the &#34;non&#34; mention of the BTK site either. <BR>Politics? <BR>Jealosy? <BR>Strange.   <BR>I am wondering...and I am a Wichita Native...is there REALLY a Tom Voight? <BR>Could this site of been a LE setup to assist in drawing out BTK? <BR>Could it be part of evidence in any way? <BR>I am so puzzled. <BR>The book is quite interesting...many many things I did not know.  But needed a bit more editing. <BR>I am not quite finished. <BR>It is just so strange that there is no communication from Tom. NONE <BR>Ah DoDah... this book is really bringing up old memories. <BR>THE Foundry.  I used to work there way back then.  Right up the street on Douglas was a Gay bar...The Flaming Mamies... <BR>The guy/girls frequented the Foundry every day...then off to work the he/shes would go. <BR>Little did I know there were undercover cops in there... <BR>Drugs were rampid. <BR>Anyways...I am off topic a little...disciplining myself<IMG SRC="http://www.catchbtk.com/discus/clipart/happy.gif" ALT=":-&#41;" BORDER=0> <BR>Chow<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12991-->
<!--Post: 12996--><!--Time: 1113926157-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12996"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 8:55 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Beattie commented that he was communicating with Runes and other on the C&amp;J board. Since he was friends with them, perhaps that is the reason. I learned a few new things from the book and it cleared up some questions. It left alot more questions. Too bad we cannot all show up for a hearing. Since he waived today&#39;s hearing, we might have been left up the creek without a serial killer. Rader should have seen the Monk episode where the bomber used a temporary glue and stuck a letter to the upper/inner side of the mail box, never knowing when it would fall into the rest of the mail and be sent. A communication might have been received while he was incarcerated, thus giving him so much reasonable doubt as to help his case. Guess he was not that smart.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12996-->
<!--Post: 12997--><!--Time: 1113926676-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST12997"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Rocky<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Rocky<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, April 19, 2005 - 9:04 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Else - the non-mention of this site is Beatty&#39;s loss, as far as I&#39;m concerned. There are some bright, professional, thought provoking people here. I know for me, it certainly has helped me to understand a lot of things about serial killers, not to mention the fact I don&#39;t feel like such a ding dong after learning of DTDC living down the street. <BR> <BR>BTW - I had my senior pics taken at the Foundry and the lil park across the street on Douglas.  My brother-in-law used to play at the Foundry on Friday nights, and we lived for draggin Douglas. &#40;I lost an oil pan or two tryin to fend them off my girlfriends and I&#41;. Yee Hah - Man, those were the days.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 12997-->
<!--Post: 13087--><!--Time: 1114131907-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13087"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 6:05 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I completed reading Beattie&#39;s book and found the analysis and depth of research to be superb. There were several flaws in the narrative ranging from the disjointed literary style to grammatical imperfections and spelling errors. Edmund Kemper was referred to as Edward Kemper on several occasions. A critical examination of the final draft would have discovered the literary faults. It would appear Beattie and the publisher compromised style for economics and the result was a C grade performance. <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13087-->
<!--Post: 13091--><!--Time: 1114151053-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13091"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, April 21, 2005 - 11:24 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Beattie&#39;s book is the type of book that I do not enjoy but I bought and read it anyway.  .  .  and I did not enjoy it. <BR> <BR>He stated that his book was a book that would outline the events of the case.  .  .  nothing more. He was right in the fact that he added nothing in regards to insight or hypothesis. <BR> <BR>Where the book fails in my opinion is the ability to report objectively the events and the abilities of the people involved in the case. The author seemed to be far to close to some reporters and some investigators. <BR> <BR>I got the feeling he was far less than objective.  .  .  that was bad but far worse was his hitting the reader over the head with his resume ! <BR> <BR>The tone of the book, in the late going, took on a back slapping &#34;aint I good&#34; quality.  .  . <BR> <BR>There are good facts in the book, so I am told by people far more knowledgeable about the case than I am, but it is difficult to accept his chain of events when he spends a good portion of the book hyping the &#34;reputation&#34; and &#34;effectiveness&#34; of the WPD.  .  .   <BR> <BR>A few people who are close to this case, and they will remain nameless so hold the question, contend that WPD was as ineffective and incompetent as some of the Bay Area PD&#39;s that never caught the Zodiac.  .  .   <BR> <BR>As Beattie tells it the WPD made the capture. In reality Dennis Rader captured himself pure and simple. The police were no closer to DR until he made his huge mistake.  .  .  this was not some feat of detective genius.  .  . it was luck pure luck.  .  .   <BR> <BR>I respect all LE and the dangers they face in everyday life. I also am grateful for their presence most of the time.  .  .   <BR> <BR>But when they screw up by not releasing information or refusing to follow psychologist recommendations then they deserve the scrutiny.  .  .   <BR> <BR>  <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13091-->
<!--Post: 13092--><!--Time: 1114154941-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13092"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Steve_huff<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Steve_huff<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 12:29 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t wish to swell your head, Ivy, but I always get a kick out of it when you and I see eye-to-eye 100%. Your assessment of &#34;Nightmare&#34; is almost the same as mine. I would, perhaps, have given it a B, if only because there were some moments in the narrative I liked, and I felt like the good writer in Beattie was obscured by poor support from the people doing the final vetting of the book for press.  <BR> <BR>Tac&#39;s words about it got me to thinking, though, and I realized that what I wanted was for the book to &#39;settle&#39; into one voice. Either take the personal, almost &#39;I was pretty much there&#39; approach the entire time, or step back for the entire narrative and be objective and comprehensive throughout. The mix of Beattie&#39;s more deeply personal observations didn&#39;t blend well with the reportorial moments.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13092-->
<!--Post: 13099--><!--Time: 1114181073-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13099"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 7:44 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Tac...you wrote EXACTLY how I also feel about the book.  I almost went back to count the number of times he gave himself kudos or indicated someone else did.  I do know that every single chapter had kudos for himself..and that if it wasn&#39;t for him BTK would not have been caught. <BR>I disagree with that assumption...I do feel that the publishing and announcement of his book in progress helped...but I think ALOT of things brought BTK out.  This site and several others...his kids were grown and gone...It has been rumored that perhaps the s.o.b. is ill and one of the profiles indicated that SOMETHING changed in his life recently, a loss of a loved one, an illness, perhaps even remorse.  I don&#39;t buy the remorse. <BR>But given BTK&#39;s age, his kids gone, his desire to be a cop and never being accepted, AND perhaps an illness...and then again the sites on the internet, all the discussion, the press, the pressure, alot of things brought the evil out of hiding. <BR>There is a part at the end of Beatties book talking about an officer receiving a gold star...makes me think of his note from jail that was posted here and now that part is gone...He said..Maybe I will recieve a gold star...something to that affect.. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13099-->
<!--Post: 13114--><!--Time: 1114197177-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13114"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Foxfire<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Foxfire<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 12:12 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->In Chat the other night on Crime &amp; Justice, he said this: <BR> <BR>&#34; In March 2004 an article I wrote was published in the RETIRED COPPER. That was sent to Park City Hall. That may have prompted the fist communication.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13114-->
<!--Post: 13115--><!--Time: 1114197956-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13115"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Elsewhere<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Elsewhere<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 12:25 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I would like to see the article.  He just cannot stop praising himself for catching DTDC. <BR>The book was good and informative..but he sure likes himself. <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13115-->
<!--Post: 13116--><!--Time: 1114201116-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13116"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 1:18 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->i have one question for those who&#39;ve read it: <BR> <BR>does he mention the Fager murders?  does he mention the supposed letter that was sent to the Fager widow?  if either is the case, what does he say about them? <BR> <BR>thanks! <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13116-->
<!--Post: 13117--><!--Time: 1114202190-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13117"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tulip<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tulip<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->3-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 1:36 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Suspiria, <BR> <BR>He does mention the Fagers and the letter from BTK.  <BR> <BR>He stated the Butterworth&#39;s DNA and the DNA found in the hot tub did not match.  His description of the prosecution&#39;s case made their &#40;the prosecution&#39;s&#41; story very hard to believe.  It was my impression that he did not believe Bill Butterworth to be guilty, although he does not state this outright. <BR> <BR>He does say that those who saw the Fager letter that he interviewed believed it to be authentic.  He seemed to believe the letter was from BTK. <BR> <BR>He does say who else he thinks may be a BTK victim.  He does not list the Fagers.  He thinks Sherry Baker &#40;and Hedge and Davis --book was mainly written prior to arrest&#41;.  In an onlince chat he also said that based on the personal similarities between Rader and Denise Rathbun, she also may be a possible BTK victim, but he didn&#39;t put much emphasis on that. <BR> <BR>Hope this is what you wanted to know.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13117-->
<!--Post: 13121--><!--Time: 1114214198-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13121"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Ivy_leaguer<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->10-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, April 22, 2005 - 4:56 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Steve, <BR> <BR>I appreciate the praise. Beattie&#39;s analysis and research cannot revive the poor literary style of the text. The C grade may seem harsh, but it was based on college assessment of compositions. College instructors rarely awarded students a grade higher than a C for a composition demonstrating extensive and exhaustive analysis and research, but lacked exceptional grammar, correct spelling and a coherent and thoughtful argument. Poor literary style hinders the author&#39;s argument and is not a pleasant experience for the audience.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13121-->
<!--Post: 13134--><!--Time: 1114271959-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST13134"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Suspiria_2<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Suspiria_2<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, April 23, 2005 - 8:59 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->thank you, Tulip!~ <BR> <BR>guess i should&#39;ve said &#34;one issue to clear up&#34;. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 13134-->
